A||||5||mrfixit58||Power Window / Mirror Switch Plate Letters||||2||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||09-26-2000||03:53 PM||||I'm trying to get ready for a local show next month and have been busy taking care of the little things in the interior. One of the items that need "freashing up" is the window/mirror switchplate. The white letters are almost completely worn off (ya know... UP, DN, POWER MIRROR). I checked with my local GM parts man and he inticated that they were no longer available from GM. Are these reproduced anywhere?<P>If no one is making these, does anyone have any ideas how to re-apply the missing lettering? Same goes for the arrows on the mirror switch. I can buy it new with a black toggle button but the original had a gray toggle button with arrows. Any ideas?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||2
Z||000001||Phil||09-26-2000||06:54 PM||||There was a pic on the Forum a fews weeks back where someone just removed the up-down lettering and replaced the window switches with ones that open/close pictures on the buttons (I like it)||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-26-2000||07:54 PM||||That was Bill Osmer. It's a complete button replacement and the buttons are litted I think. not just relabled or the covers replaced. He's got an article in the works that explains what he did.<P>There are rub on lettering sets available in many stationary/office supply stores. Try Helvetica font. Should be real close. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||jmw||09-26-2000||10:43 PM||||I don't know if you'd be interested or not - this may be too cheesy for you - especially if yours is a show car, but I just did a whole project where I made a bunch of  interior labels for lots of stuff, including the power window switches (I was pretty proud of myself for figuring out how to draw a little key on the computer - now I know Rodney Dickman sells them). I also did some teeny-tiny triangles for the power mirror switch. For the lettering, I used Arial font. The lettering sizes vary. Just laser printed them a page of sticky labels, covered them with clear packing tape, cut them out and stuck them on. They don't look fantastic - I mean you can sort of tell they're stickers if you look up close, but in my case they're better than nothing. If you want I would be glad to send you some stickers to try out, or email you the document they're in (I used black background, but I'm sure you could match the gray shade to your interior). If you didn't like them you could just chuck them. I will be out of town all next week, but if you want them after that let me know. The other thing I was going to try is a fine-point white marker. Office supply stores have them, but you don't want the rollerball point, you want the felt-tip kind. I would imagine you'd have to have a pretty darn steady hand to get the lettering perfect, but it is another idea at least (I tried a white-out pen and it didn't work, that's how I know about the rollerball thing). I will be interested to know if / what you find that works. Thanks. Sorry this is so long. I am bad about that.||||reg||7
Z||000004||mrfixit58||09-27-2000||08:17 AM||||phil - I saw the thread about the switches and it looked interesting. It would be pretty nice if the switches were illuminated like the power trunk switch (orange). <P>jmw - Thanks for the offer. My car isn't exactly a show car, Just a clean daily driver type. I'm trying to keep the look mostly original.<P>ogre - Thanks for the "heads-up" on the stationary store. Now that you mention it, I seem to remember some rub-off decales at the electronics store, too.<P>I'll keep everyone posted.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||peterh||09-27-2000||08:27 AM||||I think those parts are still available from the Fiero Store.<BR>||||reg||1
